  Merge rationale:
  - p1: three diagrams of ONE task set under ONE set of tie-break conventions. Split
    three ways, three workers would invent three conventions and the diagrams would
    disagree with each other.
  - p2: 2.2's whole argument is "every creation path reduces to a running process
    calling fork()+exec()"; 2.3 is that mechanism as a program. Same worker keeps the
    argument and the code the same shape.
  - p4: a zombie IS the state exit() leaves the child in and waitpid() reaps. Split,
    the two answers would describe the exit/wait handshake differently.
  - p5/p6: one exercise each, three papers each; kept whole so the three critiques
    share a voice, and so p6 can be told explicitly to avoid the seven list papers
    that belong to p5. Cross-problem overlap is exactly what a worker validator
    cannot see, so it is pinned in the briefs.
  Not merged: p3 (Ex 2.4) is about context-switch cost, a different subject from
  process creation; bundling it into p2 would be bundling to save a spawn.

  Nothing uncovered.

